Branch-local material for REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW ... WHERE ...
================================================================

This directory is NOT part of any test schedule and is not wired into either
build system.  Delete it before posting the patch to -hackers.  It holds the
two things that do not belong in the patch itself:

  ISSUES.md  Open review items, split into those raised on the -hackers thread
             and those found separately, each mapped to the test that covers it.

  run.sh     The stress reproducer described below.

The reproducer exists because one of the failure modes of the non-concurrent
partial refresh cannot be expressed deterministically as an isolation spec.

The row-locking step of refresh_by_direct_modification() is

    SELECT 1 FROM <matview> mv WHERE (<predicate>) FOR UPDATE

with no ORDER BY, so a single refresh statement locks rows in whatever order its
plan happens to produce.  Two concurrent refreshes whose predicates select
overlapping rows but produce different plans will therefore lock those rows in
different orders and deadlock.  Reproducing that requires both statements to be
simultaneously mid-scan, which isolationtester cannot arrange -- it drives one
step at a time.

src/test/isolation/specs/matview-where-deadlock.spec covers the deterministic,
cross-statement variant of the same flaw.  Note that adding ORDER BY to the
locking SELECT fixes the variant reproduced here but not the one in that spec:
ordering within one statement says nothing about the order of separate
statements.

Usage
-----

    ./run.sh [PGPORT] [PGHOST] [PGDATABASE]

Defaults: port 5432, host /tmp, database postgres.  Requires psql on PATH.

The script builds a matview whose physical row order is the reverse of its key
order, then runs two sessions in parallel:

  - one refreshing with a predicate the planner satisfies with an index scan,
    which locks in ascending key order;
  - one refreshing with a predicate that forces a sequential scan, which locks
    in physical order, i.e. descending key order.

It exits non-zero and prints a count if any deadlock is detected.  On an
unfixed tree roughly half the refreshes abort.
